  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 339 AM EDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nPattern / Forecast Synopsis: \n\nRex block coinciding with strong surface high over Quebec will allow \nfor further blocking to materialize with a developing nor'easter set \nto move through the 40/70 benchmark off the mid Atlantic coast and \nlargely stall out. Expanding influence of this system will lead to \nthe rex block and surface high to retrograde west into the Great \nLakes into next week... essentially reducing any incoming shortwaves \nto some increased cloud cover overhead owing to minimal moisture \nintrusion. Meanwhile, longwave troughing over the Pacific coast will \nlead to deep tropical Pacific moisture advection to manifest within \nan active subtropical jet, leading to periodic ejections of energy \ninto the central and southern Plains. The blocking and surface high \noverhead should remain steadfast, but there are some indications \nthat just enough of this moisture may leak northward later into next \nweek to perhaps drum up some rain chances. \n\nDetails: \n\nStrong surface high pressure and subsidence will suppress most \nincoming weather concerns. A look at satellite really showcases \nthis... weak northern stream shortwave leading to thicker cloud \ncover across the pond in Wisconsin and into Minnesota, but \nstruggling mightily to expand into our neck of the woods. Already \nmeager rainfall from this system will essentially dry up before it \ngets here through the day, leading to just some increased mid to \nhigh cloud cover over the region. Once this dissipating wave clears, \nthe retrograding core of the cut off ridge and surface high pressure \nmoves overhead, and returns drier and clearer weather to the region. \nAs noted by previous forecaster... may need to watch fire weather \nparameters, but with such a meager pressure gradient, will be hard \npressed to have legitimate, headline attaining fire weather concerns \nduring this stretch of weather, despite daytime temperatures running \nabove normal into the 70s. One thing to note... will still need to \nwatch for patchy frost concerns tonight and Saturday night, though \nrecent data is trending a touch more bearish to this idea... Friday \nnight owing to a bit more of a widespread fog footprint potentially \nstonewalling falling temperatures, and Saturday night owing to less \nconfidence in a deeper, cool and dry layer within the profile to \nreally attain a diurnal temperature swing of or in excess of 35 \ndegrees between daytime high and nighttime low temps. \n\nLooking into next week, particularly midweek and beyond, moisture \nejections from the aforementioned active Pacific moisture transport \nregime within the subtropical jet will make an attempt to drive rain \nchances back into the region. With periods of ridging overhead \nthrough much of the week and a weaker northern stream flow with \nminimal shortwave action, I do have a hard time seeing meaningful \nrain chances reaching far enough north into the region to have \nanything more than a scattered rain coverage. Split flow patterns \nare pretty common within strong El Nino regimes, and pattern \nrecognition says that a stationary front currently draped across the \nsouthern Plains into the Gulf States will be the focus for a \nmajority of this activity, and sops up the deepest moisture \nadvection in the eastern two thirds of the CONUS. Furthermore, \nwithout any potent wave ejections to drive LLJ manifestation, \nanticipate that the northward advance of deeper moisture leaves much \nto be desired until late in the week, and beyond the forecast period \ninto next weekend. As such... will hold off on any aggressive rain \nmessaging into next week, as this setup remains rather unclear at \nthis juncture.",
